course: dictionary
lesson: 15332
title: Two meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Snook is one of them. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer means. «Tom cocked a snook at the bully and walked on.» Cock a snook is a rude, mocking sign, so the sentence is about a gesture, not a fish.
explain: «The angler hauled a long, silvery snook onto the boat.» Here snook is a fish. The clues are angler, silvery and boat. Ask yourself: what would fit here, a fish or a rude sign? A fish fits, so that is the meaning.
explain: Check your choice by swapping in a meaning. Does the sentence still make sense? A rude sign does not fit with hauled onto a boat, so the fish meaning is right. When the clues are unclear, use a dictionary.
fact: Snook has two meanings: a fish, and the phrase «cock a snook».
fact: «Cock a snook» (UK, old-fashioned) means to make a rude, mocking sign by putting your thumb on your nose and spreading your fingers.
fact: «Cock a snook at» someone means to cheekily defy or mock them, like breaking a silly rule on purpose.
fact: A snook is a long, silvery fish of warm seas, caught for food and sport.
fact: Anglers in the Caribbean and Florida fish for snook.
exercise: «Tom cocked a snook at the bully and walked on.» What does cock a snook mean here? | Cooked a fish for dinner | *Made a rude, mocking sign at someone | Waved a friendly hello | Ran away very fast
exercise: «The angler hauled a long, silvery snook onto the boat.» What is a snook in this sentence? | *A fish | A rude sign | A boat | A kind of rope
exercise: «Nina cocked a snook at the rule that said no running.» What does this suggest Nina did? | *Openly defied the rule on purpose | Asked the teacher a question | Forgot the rule by accident | Obeyed the rule very carefully
test: «Snook live in warm seas, and anglers love to catch them.» What kind of place is the home of the snook? | Snowy mountains | Dry desert rivers | *Warm seas | Cold icy lakes
test: Ben says cock a snook is old-fashioned, so he will never use it. Why might a writer still use it? | Because it means to fall asleep | Because it is the name of a cake | Because it means exactly the same as hello | *Because it is a lively old phrase for cheeky defiance
exam: «Ella cocked a snook at her brother, then giggled.» Which word before snook shows it is part of a phrase and not a fish? | brother | Ella | giggled | *cocked
